# macOS Swift Development Rules

## Swift Language Best Practices

### Code Style & Organization
- Use Swift naming conventions: PascalCase for types, camelCase for variables/functions
- Prefer `let` over `var` unless mutation is required
- Use `guard` statements for early returns and unwrapping optionals
- Prefer `if let` or `guard let` over force unwrapping (`!`)
- Use `nil` coalescing operator (`??`) for default values
- Mark classes as `final` unless subclassing is intended
- Use `private` access control by default, escalate only when needed
- Group related code with `// MARK: -` comments
- Keep functions focused and single-purpose (SRP)
- Prefer computed properties over methods when appropriate

### Optionals & Safety
- Never force unwrap (`!`) unless absolutely certain of non-nil value
- Use optional chaining (`?.`) and nil coalescing (`??`) appropriately
- Prefer `guard let` for early returns in functions
- Use `if let` for optional binding when you need the value in a scope
- Consider `Result<Success, Failure>` for operations that can fail

### Memory Management
- Use `weak` references for delegates and parent references to avoid retain cycles
- Use `unowned` only when you're certain the reference will outlive the object
- Be mindful of closures capturing `self` - use `[weak self]` or `[unowned self]` when appropriate
- Use `@StateObject` for view-owned ObservableObjects
- Use `@ObservedObject` for externally-owned ObservableObjects
- Use `@EnvironmentObject` for shared state across view hierarchy
- Prefer value types (structs, enums) over reference types (classes) when possible

### Error Handling
- Use `throws` and `do-catch` for recoverable errors
- Use `Result<Success, Failure>` for operations that can fail
- Prefer specific error types over generic `Error`
- Use `try?` only when you genuinely don't care about the error
- Log errors appropriately for debugging

### Concurrency
- Use `async/await` for asynchronous operations (Swift 5.5+)
- Use `@MainActor` for UI updates and main thread operations
- Use `Task` for fire-and-forget async work
- Use `Task.detached` for background work that doesn't need main actor
- Prefer `async let` for parallel operations
- Use `actor` for thread-safe state management
- Avoid blocking the main thread with synchronous operations

## SwiftUI Best Practices

### View Composition
- Break down large views into smaller, reusable components
- Use `@ViewBuilder` for conditional view construction
- Prefer composition over complex conditional logic in views
- Extract view modifiers into reusable extensions
- Use `Group` or `@ViewBuilder` for multiple views without a container

### State Management
- Use `@State` for local view state
- Use `@StateObject` for view-owned ObservableObjects
- Use `@ObservedObject` for externally-owned ObservableObjects
- Use `@EnvironmentObject` for shared app-wide state
- Use `@AppStorage` for UserDefaults-backed state
- Use `@Binding` for two-way data flow between parent and child views
- Minimize state - derive values from state when possible

### Performance
- Use `id()` modifier for stable identity when needed
- Use `LazyVStack`/`LazyHStack` for large lists
- Avoid creating views in body - extract to computed properties or methods
- Use `onAppear`/`onDisappear` for lifecycle hooks
- Prefer `equatable()` modifier for expensive views
- Use `@StateObject` initialization in `init()` when possible

### View Updates
- Keep view bodies pure - no side effects
- Perform side effects in `.onAppear`, `.onChange`, or `.task`
- Use `.task(id:)` for async work tied to view lifecycle
- Use `.onChange(of:)` for reacting to state changes
- Avoid performing work in view body

## macOS-Specific Patterns

### AppKit Integration
- Use `NSViewRepresentable` for AppKit views when SwiftUI doesn't suffice
- Use `NSViewControllerRepresentable` for AppKit view controllers
- Implement proper coordinator pattern for delegates
- Handle AppKit lifecycle methods appropriately (`makeNSView`, `updateNSView`)
- Use `NSApplicationDelegateAdaptor` for app delegate functionality

### Window Management
- Use `WindowGroup` for standard windows
- Use `.windowStyle()` for window appearance
- Handle window lifecycle with appropriate modifiers
- Use `NSWindow` APIs when needed for advanced window features
- Respect macOS window management conventions

### Menu & Commands
- Use `CommandGroup` for menu customization
- Provide standard keyboard shortcuts (Cmd+T, Cmd+W, etc.)
- Use `.keyboardShortcut()` modifier for shortcuts
- Follow macOS Human Interface Guidelines for menu organization
- Use `CommandMenu` for custom menus

### Settings
- Use `Settings` scene for preferences window
- Use `TabView` for multi-pane settings
- Persist settings with `@AppStorage` or `UserDefaults`
- Provide sensible defaults
- Validate user input in settings

### File System & Permissions
- Request appropriate entitlements in `.entitlements` file
- Use `FileManager` for file operations
- Handle sandbox restrictions appropriately
- Request user permission for sensitive operations
- Use URL-based APIs when possible

## Architecture Patterns

### MVVM (Model-View-ViewModel)
- Separate business logic into ViewModels (ObservableObject)
- Keep Views focused on presentation
- Models should be data structures, not business logic
- Use dependency injection for testability

### Singleton Pattern
- Use sparingly - prefer dependency injection
- When needed, use `static let shared = ClassName()`
- Make initializer `private` to enforce singleton
- Consider thread safety for shared state

### Coordinator Pattern
- Use for navigation and complex flows
- Keep navigation logic out of views
- Use for AppKit delegate coordination

## Code Organization

### File Structure
- One type per file (with exceptions for closely related types)
- Group related files in folders (Models, Views, ViewModels, Utilities)
- Use extensions to organize code within files
- Keep file sizes manageable (< 500 lines when possible)

### Naming
- Use descriptive, intention-revealing names
- Avoid abbreviations unless widely understood
- Use verb phrases for functions, noun phrases for types
- Prefix protocols with the type they describe (e.g., `ViewDelegate`)

### Documentation
- Use `///` for public API documentation
- Document complex algorithms and business logic
- Use `// MARK:` to organize code sections
- Keep comments up-to-date with code changes

## Testing

### Unit Tests
- Write tests for business logic and ViewModels
- Use `XCTest` framework
- Test edge cases and error conditions
- Mock dependencies for isolated testing
- Aim for high code coverage on critical paths

### UI Tests
- Use SwiftUI previews for rapid iteration
- Test user flows with UI tests when appropriate
- Use `#Preview` macro for SwiftUI previews
- Test on different screen sizes and configurations

## Performance & Optimization

### Build Performance
- Use `private` to enable whole-module optimization
- Minimize `@Published` properties (only publish what's needed)
- Use `@Published` sparingly - prefer manual `objectWillChange.send()`
- Avoid unnecessary view updates

### Runtime Performance
- Profile with Instruments before optimizing
- Use `lazy` for expensive computations
- Cache expensive operations
- Minimize work on main thread
- Use appropriate data structures for performance-critical code

## Dependencies & Package Management

### Swift Package Manager
- Prefer SPM over CocoaPods when possible
- Pin dependencies to specific versions
- Keep dependencies up-to-date for security
- Document why each dependency is needed

### External Libraries
- Minimize dependencies - prefer native APIs
- Evaluate library maintenance and community support
- Consider licensing implications
- Document external dependencies

## Security & Privacy

### Data Protection
- Use Keychain for sensitive data
- Encrypt sensitive data at rest
- Use secure network communication (HTTPS)
- Validate and sanitize user input
- Follow macOS privacy guidelines

### Permissions
- Request permissions only when needed
- Explain why permissions are needed
- Handle permission denials gracefully
- Respect user privacy choices

## Common Pitfalls to Avoid

- Don't create ObservableObjects in view body - use `@StateObject`
- Don't perform heavy work on main thread
- Don't ignore errors - handle them appropriately
- Don't use force unwrapping without certainty
- Don't create retain cycles with strong references
- Don't update UI from background threads
- Don't store large objects in `@Published` unnecessarily
- Don't create views conditionally without proper identity
- Don't ignore memory warnings
- Don't block the main thread

## Code Review Checklist

- [ ] No force unwraps without justification
- [ ] Proper access control (private by default)
- [ ] No retain cycles
- [ ] Main thread for UI updates
- [ ] Error handling in place
- [ ] Appropriate use of async/await
- [ ] Views are composable and reusable
- [ ] State management follows SwiftUI patterns
- [ ] Follows macOS HIG where applicable
- [ ] Performance considerations addressed

